 "section_summary": "This section contains portions of two statistical reports on Japanese educational institutions compiled by the Civil Information and Education Section of GHQ SCAP as of April and November 1946. Pages 119-153 conclude a report on Iwate Prefecture, providing rosters and statistics for village, town, and private elementary, secondary, vocational, youth, and special schools, and ending with a reference map of Northern Honshu, though it includes an extraneous leaf (page 122) listing Ibaraki prefectural middle schools filmed in place of page 60. Pages 154-175 comprise the initial portion of Special Report AR-302-E-A-26-14 (Part 14: Ibaraki Prefecture, dated August 28, 1948), including its title page, table of contents, introduction explaining educational control jurisdictions and survey methodology, classification charts, and prefectural summary tables 1 through 10.",
 "subfiles": [
  {
   "about": "A statistical roster and directory of Japanese educational institutions in Iwate Prefecture detailing foundation dates, student enrollments, and teaching personnel as of April and November 1946.",
   "continues_previous": true,
   "date": "",
   "doc_type": "report",
   "end_page": 153,
   "from": "Education Unit, Research Branch, Analysis and Research Division, Civil Information and Education Section, GHQ, SCAP",
   "keywords": [
    "Iwate Prefecture",
    "elementary schools",
    "private schools",
    "youth schools",
    "kindergartens",
    "vocational schools",
    "Northern Honshu",
    "school directory"
   ],
   "start_page": 119,
   "summary": "This report concludes a comprehensive statistical roster of educational institutions in Iwate Prefecture based on official school records as of April 30 and November 30, 1946. It details village and town elementary schools and kindergartens, private colleges, middle schools, girls' high schools, vocational schools, youth schools, and elementary institutions, as well as specialized facilities under non-educational government ministries, ending with a reference map of Northern Honshu. Note that page 122 is an erroneously inserted page (page 6) from the Ibaraki Prefecture roster listing prefectural middle schools, filmed in place of page 60 of the Iwate roster.",
   "title": "Roster of Japanese Educational Institutions: Iwate Prefecture",
   "to": ""
  },
  {
   "about": "The introductory text, administrative classifications, and statistical summary tables of a SCAP CI&E special report surveying educational institutions in Ibaraki Prefecture.",
   "continues_previous": false,
   "date": "1948-08-28",
   "doc_type": "report",
   "end_page": 175,
   "from": "Education Unit, Research Branch, Analysis and Research Division, Civil Information and Education Section, GHQ, SCAP",
   "keywords": [
    "Ibaraki Prefecture",
    "SCAP",
    "Civil Information and Education Section",
    "educational statistics",
    "school census",
    "control jurisdictions",
    "educational administration"
   ],
   "start_page": 154,
   "summary": "This document comprises the front matter, introductory text, and aggregate statistical tables of Special Report AR-302-E-A-26-14 (Part 14: Ibaraki Prefecture), prepared between December 1947 and August 1948 from school records as of April 30 and November 30, 1946. The introduction details the pre-reform structure of Japanese schooling, the division of administrative authority between national, public, and private control jurisdictions, and the survey methodology used to compile the 47-part national roster. Summary tables 1 through 10 provide quantitative breakdowns of educational institutions, student enrollments by sex, and full-time and part-time teaching personnel categorized by level, institutional type, and administrative jurisdiction.",
   "title": "Roster of Japanese Educational Institutions and Statistical Tables - Part 14, Ibaraki Prefecture (Special Report AR-302-E-A-26-14)",
   "to": ""
  }
